---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';

const path = '/cm/';

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'How many millimeters are in a centimeter?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Ten. A centimeter is defined as exactly 10 millimeters, and the on-screen ruler shows this directly: ten small ticks between every pair of numbered centimeter marks.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'How many inches is a centimeter?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'One centimeter equals exactly 0.3937 inches (one inch is defined as exactly 2.54 cm). So 10 cm is about 3.94 inches — just under four inches.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'What everyday objects are about one centimeter?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'A standard pencil is about 0.7 cm across, a US penny is about 1.9 cm across, and the width of an adult fingertip is roughly 1.5–2 cm. A paperclip is about 3 cm long and a credit card is 8.56 cm wide.',
      },
    },
  ],
};

const related = [
  { href: '/mm/', label: 'Millimeter ruler' },
  { href: '/inches/', label: 'Inch ruler' },
  { href: '/guide/', label: 'How to read the ruler' },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Online Centimeter Ruler — Measure in cm at True Size | Real Online Ruler"
  description="A free on-screen centimeter ruler at true physical size. Calibrate once, then measure in cm and mm with numbered centimeter marks and millimeter ticks."
  path={path}
  h1="Centimeter ruler"
  lede="The centimeter is the workhorse of everyday metric measuring — big enough to read at a glance, fine enough for most household tasks. Calibrate your display once, switch the ruler to cm, and the numbered marks on your screen are true centimeters."
  breadcrumb="Centimeter ruler"
  jsonLd={[faqJsonLd]}
  related={related}
>
  <h2>What is a centimeter?</h2>
  <p>
    A centimeter is one hundredth of a meter — about the width of an adult fingertip. It sits in the
    sweet spot for everyday measuring: smaller than an inch (2.54 cm), larger than fiddly
    sub-millimeter units. Most of the world measures daily life in centimeters: heights, paper
    sizes, furniture, screen diagonals.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading the cm scale</h2>
  <p>
    In cm mode the <strong>long numbered ticks are centimeters</strong> — 1, 2, 3, and so on.
    Between each pair sit nine shorter ticks: <strong>millimeters</strong>. The medium-length tick
    halfway between is the half centimeter (5 mm). An object ending at the third short tick past 7
    measures 7.3 cm. If you need the value purely in millimeters, switch to
    <a href="/mm/">mm mode</a> and read 73 mm directly.
  </p>

  <h2>Handy size references</h2>
  <p>Everyday objects, for sanity-checking your calibration or estimating without the ruler:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Standard paperclip — about <strong>3 cm</strong> long
    </li>
    <li>
      AA battery — about <strong>5 cm</strong> long
    </li>
    <li>
      Credit / bank card — <strong>8.56 cm</strong> wide (an exact standard, great for checking
      calibration)
    </li>
    <li>
      US penny — about <strong>1.9 cm</strong> across
    </li>
    <li>
      Smartphone width — typically <strong>7–8 cm</strong>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Conversions</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>From</th>
        <th>To</th>
        <th>Multiply by</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>cm</td>
        <td>mm</td>
        <td>10</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>cm</td>
        <td>m</td>
        <td>0.01</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>cm</td>
        <td>inches</td>
        <td>0.3937</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>inches</td>
        <td>cm</td>
        <td>2.54 (exact)</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The inch conversion is exact by definition — one inch is <em>defined</em> as 2.54 cm — so
    switching the ruler between cm and <a href="/inches/">inches</a> never introduces rounding error
    in the tick positions themselves.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>How many millimeters are in a centimeter?</h3>
  <p>
    Ten. A centimeter is defined as exactly 10 millimeters, and the on-screen ruler shows this
    directly: ten small ticks between every pair of numbered centimeter marks.
  </p>
  <h3>How many inches is a centimeter?</h3>
  <p>
    One centimeter equals exactly 0.3937 inches (one inch is defined as exactly 2.54 cm). So 10 cm
    is about 3.94 inches — just under four inches.
  </p>
  <h3>What everyday objects are about one centimeter?</h3>
  <p>
    A standard pencil is about 0.7 cm across, a US penny is about 1.9 cm across, and the width of an
    adult fingertip is roughly 1.5–2 cm. A paperclip is about 3 cm long and a credit card is 8.56 cm
    wide.
  </p>
</ContentLayout>
